WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.270
Los Angeles is now coming together

00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:05.670
to confront the greatest
moral and humanitarian crisis

00:00:05.670 --> 00:00:06.560
of our time.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:11.560
Let me be clear once again, we
are here to end homelessness.

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:17.530
Five years after Los Angeles
voters approved tax increases

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.470
to raise one and a half billion dollars

00:00:19.470 --> 00:00:21.750
to address the region's
homelessness problem,

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:24.310
there are more people living
and dying on the streets

00:00:24.310 --> 00:00:25.840
than ever before.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.800
Many of these men and women
are both frequent targets

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.930
and perpetrators of violence.

00:00:30.930 --> 00:00:33.660
The mayor who did not respond
to our interview request

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:36.210
has partially blamed this
failure on the pandemic,

00:00:36.210 --> 00:00:38.020
which slowed new housing construction,

00:00:38.020 --> 00:00:39.680
and limited shelter capacity.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:42.160
It's true that COVID caused
a certain homelessness,

00:00:42.160 --> 00:00:44.530
but the city's plan was already failing.

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:48.870
In 2019, homelessness
spiked 13% in LA County.

00:00:48.870 --> 00:00:52.920
This was happening way before
the pandemic, trends popping up

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.450
places that we didn't see it before.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:56.040
It's just getting worse and worse.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:00.820
Think about any city that
has 70,000 displaced people

00:01:00.820 --> 00:01:03.410
on the streets, it's unprecedented

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:06.600
and it needs an emergency response.

00:01:06.600 --> 00:01:11.090
Everybody's looking for the
next position of their career

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.210
rather than focusing on the task at hand.

00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:18.060
The centerpiece of LA's plan
was to spend the $1.2 billion

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.520
building 10,000 supportive
housing units over a decade.

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:23.800
Even if the city were
able to pull that off,

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:25.790
it would merely put a dent in the problem.

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.310
A new housing project
opened in Los Angeles

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:31.060
as one of the first under measure HHH.

00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:33.810
We will see an opening
of one of these about

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.810
every three weeks this year.

00:01:35.810 --> 00:01:38.270
By my calculations next year,
it might be every two weeks

00:01:38.270 --> 00:01:39.330
or less.

00:01:39.330 --> 00:01:42.140
Five years into the ten-year
plan just 14 projects

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:44.630
are in service of the
promised 10,000 supportive

00:01:44.630 --> 00:01:48.240
housing units, the city has
completed fewer than 700.

00:01:48.240 --> 00:01:50.880
It would take more than 30
years to house all of the people

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.130
currently homeless in
LA County at that pace,

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.200
according to a federal court order.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:58.140
As the homeless population
exploded some shelter providers

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:00.130
implored the mayor to
spend more of the money

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.690
on immediate shelter,
mental health services,

00:02:02.690 --> 00:02:04.230
and substance abuse treatment.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.520
But Garcetti went all in on
his ambitious ten-year plan.

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.210
Los Angeles approach to
the homelessness crisis

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.310
is a series of colossal failures.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:14.980
The city has proven itself
to be incapable of solving

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:17.640
homelessness, but it could
take more modest steps

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:20.530
of helping alleviate,
suffering, and restore peace

00:02:20.530 --> 00:02:22.140
and safety to the streets.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.580
It could also bring an end
to many longstanding policies

00:02:25.580 --> 00:02:28.220
that caused these men and
women to end up homeless

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.803
in the first place.

00:02:32.170 --> 00:02:34.360
Los Angeles has the largest unsheltered

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.340
homeless population in
America and Ground Zero

00:02:37.340 --> 00:02:40.940
is a 50 square block
district known as Skid Row

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:43.070
officially turned into a containment zone

00:02:43.070 --> 00:02:45.510
by the city in 1976.

00:02:45.510 --> 00:02:48.190
The problem has escalated
into a full blown

00:02:48.190 --> 00:02:49.980
humanitarian crisis.

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:53.350
In escaping court order issued
in April Federal District

00:02:53.350 --> 00:02:55.940
Judge David Carter called
the city's inaction,

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:58.770
so egregious and the
state so non-functional

00:02:58.770 --> 00:03:00.860
that it is strongly likely in violation

00:03:00.860 --> 00:03:02.770
of the equal protection clause.

00:03:02.770 --> 00:03:04.770
A federal judge calls LA's response

00:03:04.770 --> 00:03:07.690
to the homeless crisis, structural racism.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:08.880
Federal Judge David O. Carter,

00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:11.490
he ordered both the
city and county to offer

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:14.710
some form of shelter to the
entire population of Skid Row.

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.790
It's a Problem that's rooted in misguided

00:03:16.790 --> 00:03:19.420
government policies,
decades in the making,

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:22.410
but Judge Carter's order places
blame for the city's failure

00:03:22.410 --> 00:03:25.330
to address the immediate
crisis squarely at the feet

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.700
of Mayor Garcetti.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.860
The process of making
good on the mayor's plan

00:03:28.860 --> 00:03:31.050
to build 10,000 units
of supportive housing

00:03:31.050 --> 00:03:33.430
has been rife with cost overruns delays

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:34.863
and possible corruption.

00:03:34.863 --> 00:03:38.540
We have local politicians who are in bed,

00:03:38.540 --> 00:03:42.060
who are being investigated by the feds,

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:44.250
our own city council people would,

00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:46.460
that's gonna slip the hold on me.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:48.610
Like what are we doing?

00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:52.950
Local public radio affiliate
KCRW has documented instances

00:03:52.950 --> 00:03:55.340
of corruption surrounding
the city's homelessness

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:58.230
housing plan with developers
receiving taxpayer money

00:03:58.230 --> 00:04:01.030
to build homeless housing,
reselling the properties

00:04:01.030 --> 00:04:03.450
to themselves for millions in profit.

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:05.450
Carter notes that the
improper relationship

00:04:05.450 --> 00:04:07.380
between City Hall and
real estate developers

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.110
is neither isolated nor new.

00:04:09.110 --> 00:04:11.810
And that the FBI has been
investigating the possible

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.240
corruption in City Hall since 2017,

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:16.810
a probe that has led to the
prosecution of real estate

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:18.550
consultants, political fundraisers,

00:04:18.550 --> 00:04:21.860
and even most notably former
council member, Jose Weezer,

00:04:21.860 --> 00:04:24.380
whose council district included Skid Row.

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.330
In June 2020 Weezer was
arrested by federal agents

00:04:27.330 --> 00:04:30.410
for using his position to
cover up illegal activities.

00:04:30.410 --> 00:04:33.320
They've said, let's keep the status quo

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:36.520
and allow the developers to continue

00:04:36.520 --> 00:04:39.360
because they are carrying
it out in good faith

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:40.660
and they're doing it well.

00:04:40.660 --> 00:04:43.040
Well, no, it's not being done well.

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.730
It's not being rolled out in
the way that it was promised

00:04:46.730 --> 00:04:49.480
and it's not being done in good faith.

00:04:49.480 --> 00:04:52.670
Andy Bales runs the Skid Row
based Union Rescue Mission,

00:04:52.670 --> 00:04:55.100
which runs entirely on private donations.

00:04:55.100 --> 00:04:58.330
He says the organization
shelters more than 900 people

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:00.810
a night and moves more than 900 people

00:05:00.810 --> 00:05:02.590
into permanent housing annually.

00:05:02.590 --> 00:05:04.880
He's been a fierce critic
of the city's approach,

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:07.800
which she says has been
slow, expensive, and capable

00:05:07.800 --> 00:05:10.810
of serving only a fraction
of the homeless population.

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:13.810
I wish I could say I was
shocked at the pushback

00:05:13.810 --> 00:05:17.593
from LA County and LA city,
but it's unexplainable.

00:05:19.490 --> 00:05:20.840
Federal Judge David O. Carter,

00:05:20.840 --> 00:05:23.460
he ordered both the
city and county to offer

00:05:23.460 --> 00:05:26.660
some form of shelter to the
entire population of Skid Row.

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:28.530
We're hearing they're evaluating options,

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.270
including an appeal.

00:05:30.270 --> 00:05:33.120
Judge Carter has ordered
the city to put one billion dollars

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:36.470
in escrow so that he can
monitor how that money is spent.

00:05:36.470 --> 00:05:38.670
He's also ordering the
city to provide shelter

00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:41.470
for the more than 4,600
people living on the streets

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:43.880
of Skid Row before the end of 2021.

00:05:43.880 --> 00:05:46.240
Judge Carter criticized
Garcetti for failing

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.820
to declare a state of
emergency, which he says

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:50.780
could have eliminated bureaucratic hurdles

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:51.940
to building new housing.

00:05:51.940 --> 00:05:54.750
The mayor has said,
Carter's order will derail

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:55.960
the city's plan.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:57.290
Stay out of our way.

00:05:57.290 --> 00:06:00.380
Roadblocks masquerading as
progress are the last things

00:06:00.380 --> 00:06:01.213
we need.

00:06:01.213 --> 00:06:03.760
Carter's critics accused
him of overstepping his role

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.150
and his aggressive ruling
and an Orange County

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:08.420
homelessness case led
another district judge

00:06:08.420 --> 00:06:10.700
to grant several city governments requests

00:06:10.700 --> 00:06:12.820
to remove him from their
cases on the grounds

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:13.920
that he's biased.

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.630
And the LA city attorney has
appealed Carter's ruling.

00:06:16.630 --> 00:06:19.460
LA County is asking to be
dropped from the suit altogether.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:24.250
This is a deadly status quo
and people don't just die.

00:06:24.250 --> 00:06:29.250
They die a horrific death of
assaults, and rapes, and heat,

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:33.940
and cold, and wet, it is
a brutal spiral to death

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.423
that we're leaving people in.

00:06:39.222 --> 00:06:40.850
I'm a believer in the right to housing,

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:42.630
but at the same time,
you don't have a right

00:06:42.630 --> 00:06:43.690
to every park bench.

00:06:43.690 --> 00:06:45.960
The city has an obligation to protect

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:47.170
its public right of ways.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:49.620
If someone is homeless, that is a tragedy

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.740
and we need to work on
that and get them into

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:54.180
some form of transitional housing.

00:06:54.180 --> 00:06:56.400
But even if city quickly
were to build more

00:06:56.400 --> 00:06:58.480
emergency shelters, not
all the men and women

00:06:58.480 --> 00:07:00.680
living on the streets of
Skid Row would necessarily

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.190
abandon their sidewalk
encampments to go live in them.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.750
I don't wanna live in a shelter

00:07:04.750 --> 00:07:08.640
because I might as well just
go back to jail, seriously.

00:07:08.640 --> 00:07:12.480
In 2006, a year after a homeless
woman was beaten to death

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:13.890
on the streets of Skid Row,

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:16.640
then Los Angeles Police
Department, Chief Bill Bratton,

00:07:16.640 --> 00:07:18.720
launched the safer cities initiative

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.510
and sent 50 police officers
to patrol the neighborhood

00:07:21.510 --> 00:07:24.130
and issue citations for minor infractions.

00:07:24.130 --> 00:07:26.360
Proponents pointed out
that in just a few months,

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.170
the number of people
sleeping on the streets

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:30.077
was cut almost in half.

00:07:30.077 --> 00:07:33.100
"We've broken the back of
the problem," said Bratton.

00:07:33.100 --> 00:07:35.820
But homeless activists said
that many of those people

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:37.870
just dispersed to other parts of the city

00:07:37.870 --> 00:07:40.490
and that the city was
criminalizing homelessness.

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:43.560
And the Garcetti administration
ended the program in 2015

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:46.870
when an LAPD officer
fatally shot a homeless man.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:48.400
I don't think the criminalization side

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.350
is reasonable at all.

00:07:50.350 --> 00:07:51.980
Once we start incarcerating people

00:07:51.980 --> 00:07:54.700
for quality of life issues,
then we've just increased

00:07:54.700 --> 00:07:56.890
our costs with the carceral system.

00:07:56.890 --> 00:07:58.950
We've re-traumatized people.

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:01.950
And I don't think there's
a moral reason to do that.

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:05.110
The idea that this is
criminalizing homelessness

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:06.910
is an absolute red herring.

00:08:06.910 --> 00:08:09.890
It's a total myth that you
can't both be compassionate

00:08:09.890 --> 00:08:12.163
and regulate public
spaces at the same time.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.480
Elizabeth Mitchell is
an attorney representing

00:08:15.480 --> 00:08:17.390
the LA Alliance for Human Rights.

00:08:17.390 --> 00:08:19.840
The group of downtown
business owners, residents,

00:08:19.840 --> 00:08:22.270
and homeless people who sued Los Angeles.

00:08:22.270 --> 00:08:24.840
She points out that when
judge Carter issued an order

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:26.920
in Orange County allowing city governments

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:29.940
to clear encampments, they
did so without arrests,

00:08:29.940 --> 00:08:32.900
by sending social workers to
offer shelter and mental health

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.210
and drug and treatment
services and inform those

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.010
living there that they had two weeks

00:08:37.010 --> 00:08:38.973
to move their belongings from the area.

00:08:38.973 --> 00:08:42.490
If you look at this approach
that we are advocating for,

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:44.890
we have sheltered over 4,000 people

00:08:44.890 --> 00:08:46.960
without a single citation or arrest.

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:50.180
So I don't see how that could
possibly be criminalization.

00:08:50.180 --> 00:08:52.930
Everybody agrees that law
enforcement should be an absolute

00:08:52.930 --> 00:08:55.410
last resort, but
regulation of public spaces

00:08:55.410 --> 00:08:57.980
needs to occur again for everyone.

00:08:57.980 --> 00:08:59.650
There's a danger that law enforcement

00:08:59.650 --> 00:09:01.410
could take a more aggressive approach

00:09:01.410 --> 00:09:03.070
if the city fails to act.

00:09:03.070 --> 00:09:06.410
LA County sheriff Alex Villanova
sent a team of deputies

00:09:06.410 --> 00:09:08.910
out to Venice Beach in mid
June without consulting

00:09:08.910 --> 00:09:11.850
the city council and vowed
to start clearing encampments

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:14.710
in the coming weeks with
or without city approval.

00:09:14.710 --> 00:09:17.150
Several weeks later, the
Los Angeles city council

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:19.730
on July 2nd approved new
prohibitions on camping,

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.820
near shelters, parks, elementary schools,

00:09:21.820 --> 00:09:24.050
and entrances to homes and businesses.

00:09:24.050 --> 00:09:27.940
Literally the only goal
is to get people sheltered

00:09:27.940 --> 00:09:30.470
and off of the street as fast as possible

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:32.150
throughout Los Angeles.

00:09:32.150 --> 00:09:35.850
What we can't accept is
people dying on the streets

00:09:35.850 --> 00:09:39.190
while the city bureaucracies
turnout out overpriced housing

00:09:39.190 --> 00:09:40.490
that helps too few.

00:09:40.490 --> 00:09:42.910
The suit also argues that the damage done

00:09:42.910 --> 00:09:45.330
to downtown residents and
entrepreneurs by the cities

00:09:45.330 --> 00:09:48.120
in competence constitutes
a taking of property.

00:09:48.120 --> 00:09:51.030
Daisy Suarez, the
proprietor of Desuar day spa

00:09:51.030 --> 00:09:53.230
in downtown LA agrees.

00:09:53.230 --> 00:09:55.490
We have customers that
refuse to come inside

00:09:55.490 --> 00:09:57.520
because they just
completely don't feel safe.

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:00.160
We have customers writing
very negative reviews

00:10:00.160 --> 00:10:02.500
saying like, you just gotta
be careful that massage

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:04.960
was amazing, but I would
recommend you go in there

00:10:04.960 --> 00:10:06.000
because it's dangerous.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.337
So when we have customers
calling us and saying,

00:10:08.337 --> 00:10:11.850
hey, I don't feel safe, I'm
gonna cancel my appointment,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.400
it's just so hard.

00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:16.660
Suarez is named in the suit
as is her brother Leandro,

00:10:16.660 --> 00:10:19.520
a Navy veteran in a wheelchair
who has often been unable

00:10:19.520 --> 00:10:23.040
to navigate around encampments,
blocking sidewalks.

00:10:23.040 --> 00:10:25.070
He had a lot of encounters with people

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:26.160
that were aggressive.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.730
People wouldn't move, people
wouldn't give him the path,

00:10:28.730 --> 00:10:32.040
obviously tense, and many times
we had to go and rescue him.

00:10:32.040 --> 00:10:33.910
The person took out a stick on him

00:10:33.910 --> 00:10:35.950
and threatened to hit him.

00:10:35.950 --> 00:10:37.690
She also says her two young children

00:10:37.690 --> 00:10:40.570
witnessed an attack on her
husband walking around the area,

00:10:40.570 --> 00:10:43.010
causing her to move the family 30 minutes

00:10:43.010 --> 00:10:44.150
outside of the city.

00:10:44.150 --> 00:10:45.273
With the kids in the stroller,

00:10:45.273 --> 00:10:48.130
somebody came randomly
hit him from the back

00:10:48.130 --> 00:10:50.240
he's on the floor, the kids are screaming,

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.950
knocked him down unprovoked.

00:10:52.950 --> 00:10:55.540
And after that, I was
like, I gotta get my kids

00:10:55.540 --> 00:10:57.740
to safer place this is
not the environment

00:10:57.740 --> 00:10:59.140
that I want my kids to grow.

00:11:00.440 --> 00:11:03.280
Between 2018 and 2019 violent crimes

00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:05.590
perpetrated by homeless suspects increased

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:09.680
22 and a half percent with a 48 percent increase in Skid Row

00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:11.080
in downtown LA.

00:11:11.080 --> 00:11:12.960
Violent crimes against homeless people

00:11:12.960 --> 00:11:15.930
increased 19 percent city-wide
in the same period.

00:11:15.930 --> 00:11:19.610
I've seen people get
beat up, run over tents,

00:11:19.610 --> 00:11:21.259
being burned on purpose.

00:11:21.259 --> 00:11:24.498
I just can't believe what
I see, it's unfathomable.

00:11:24.498 --> 00:11:26.230
It's really difficult to watch.

00:11:26.230 --> 00:11:29.560
Harry Tadjian is co-owner of
an upholstery supply business

00:11:29.560 --> 00:11:32.890
and Skid Row where he says he's
seen conditions deteriorate

00:11:32.890 --> 00:11:34.690
on the block outside his building.

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:39.690
After mid 2016, it changed
where the tents did not move.

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:42.150
The tents started getting
bigger and bigger.

00:11:42.150 --> 00:11:46.550
Their contents started flowing
from one side of the sidewalk

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:49.520
to the other, and now
well into the streets.

00:11:49.520 --> 00:11:53.140
We have vendors that fly
in from all over the world.

00:11:53.140 --> 00:11:54.840
They just can't believe what they see.

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:57.100
When they come to Los
Angeles, they're eager to see

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.570
the best city in the
world and they see this.

00:11:59.570 --> 00:12:00.710
They're just blown away.

00:12:00.710 --> 00:12:04.127
And I've never heard once
a customer walk in and say,

00:12:04.127 --> 00:12:06.000
yeah, this time it got better.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:09.820
It's constantly getting worse
and worse and worse and worse.

00:12:09.820 --> 00:12:12.570
Both Tadjian and Suarez trace the decline

00:12:12.570 --> 00:12:15.440
that began in 2016 to a
federal court injunction,

00:12:15.440 --> 00:12:17.470
prohibiting police from seizing property

00:12:17.470 --> 00:12:19.910
people are storing on
the sidewalk or street.

00:12:19.910 --> 00:12:21.690
What's happened over the last 20 years

00:12:21.690 --> 00:12:24.200
is through these series of
decisions and settlements.

00:12:24.200 --> 00:12:26.410
That's had a complete
chilling effect on city

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:27.800
and county officials.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.930
Federal court rulings and
settlements have shaped

00:12:29.930 --> 00:12:32.200
homeless policy, not only in Los Angeles,

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:35.360
but nationwide with the February, 2021,

00:12:35.360 --> 00:12:38.370
Boise V Martin settlement,
making clear that cities

00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:41.870
couldn't clear encampments
without offering adequate shelter

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:44.260
a term that's never
been precisely defined.

00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:46.120
So the question was by the Alliance,

00:12:46.120 --> 00:12:49.050
is there a way that we can use
the courts to do the opposite

00:12:49.050 --> 00:12:52.707
to un-handcuff the county of
Los Angeles and the officials

00:12:54.300 --> 00:12:56.440
to really do what they
say they want to do,

00:12:56.440 --> 00:12:58.500
which will solve this comprehensively?

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:00.960
Mitchell says, Judge
Carter's order gives the city

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:03.970
an opportunity to bypass
this morass of settlements

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:06.040
and begin to move people off the sidewalks

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:07.530
and into temporary shelter.

00:13:07.530 --> 00:13:09.710
But instead of embracing that opportunity,

00:13:09.710 --> 00:13:11.820
Garcetti has appealed that decision.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:15.280
The mayor is just kind of
gaslighting us in a way.

00:13:15.280 --> 00:13:17.490
I have no respect for the Mayor Garcetti.

00:13:17.490 --> 00:13:21.380
I absolutely think that
he is the disgrace.

00:13:21.380 --> 00:13:24.120
His usage of funds from HHH

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:26.440
is just unbelievable, ridiculous.

00:13:26.440 --> 00:13:30.810
Our city, our county, LA Times 
have all pushed back

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:34.290
and fought the judge and
made disingenuous claims

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.520
that we can't put a roof
over everybody's heads

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:37.353
in three months.

00:13:37.353 --> 00:13:39.800
Well, I can guarantee you if you appeal

00:13:39.800 --> 00:13:42.490
and take it to the ninth circuit court

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:44.140
and to the Supreme court.

00:13:44.140 --> 00:13:46.873
Yeah, you can't get it
done if you're appealing.

00:13:48.840 --> 00:13:51.030
In Judge Carter's report,
he also criticized

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:52.840
Mayor Garcetti for his failure to spend

00:13:52.840 --> 00:13:55.860
a significant portion of the 
1.2 billion dollars that the city

00:13:55.860 --> 00:13:58.560
has borrowed on constructing
temporary shelters

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:01.190
like the sprung structures, tiny houses,

00:14:01.190 --> 00:14:02.930
or even 3D printed homes.

00:14:02.930 --> 00:14:05.030
The Garcetti administration
claims to have moved

00:14:05.030 --> 00:14:07.260
more than 30,000 people
into permanent housing

00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:09.180
using existing housing stock.

00:14:09.180 --> 00:14:10.820
But the growth of LA is unsheltered.

00:14:10.820 --> 00:14:13.240
Homeless population is far outpacing

00:14:13.240 --> 00:14:14.540
new housing construction.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.850
It was working with Judge
Carter and hearing a lot

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:18.200
about these tiny homes.

00:14:18.200 --> 00:14:20.510
We started doing some research
on them and they seemed like

00:14:20.510 --> 00:14:23.560
a good option to add to the mix.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.930
Bob Blumenfield is a city council member

00:14:25.930 --> 00:14:28.000
whose district in the San Fernando Valley

00:14:28.000 --> 00:14:30.310
has a relatively small homeless population

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.070
compared to the rest of LA.

00:14:32.070 --> 00:14:35.060
After consulting with judge
Carter, he was able to secure

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:38.320
funding to build 52
tiny homes with 100 beds

00:14:38.320 --> 00:14:40.140
in the parking lot behind his office

00:14:40.140 --> 00:14:42.860
administered by a nonprofit
that will provide meals,

00:14:42.860 --> 00:14:45.990
amenities like laundry,
internet access, storage, drug,

00:14:45.990 --> 00:14:48.630
and mental health counseling,
and 24/7 security.

00:14:48.630 --> 00:14:52.230
Temporary shelter is costly,
but far cheaper and quicker

00:14:52.230 --> 00:14:54.570
to construct than permanent
supportive housing

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:58.430
which is averaging about
550,000 dollars per unit in LA.

00:14:58.430 --> 00:15:02.400
The total cost of this cabin
community was 3.1 million dollars

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.880
or 31,000 dollars per bed.

00:15:04.880 --> 00:15:06.430
The perfect is often
the enemy of the good.

00:15:06.430 --> 00:15:08.450
Yes, it would be
wonderful to put everybody

00:15:08.450 --> 00:15:10.690
into permanent supportive
housing, that's the goal.

00:15:10.690 --> 00:15:14.040
But when you look at the numbers
that is not gonna happen.

00:15:14.040 --> 00:15:16.080
We can't tell people that until we have

00:15:16.080 --> 00:15:17.440
all the hermit supportive housing,

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:19.510
that it's okay to die on the streets

00:15:19.510 --> 00:15:23.143
or to live on the streets
in horrible conditions.

00:15:24.250 --> 00:15:26.210
Bloomfield's cabin community model

00:15:26.210 --> 00:15:28.930
is based on a similar idea
launched in Riverside County

00:15:28.930 --> 00:15:32.250
last year at about a third of
the construction cost per bed.

00:15:32.250 --> 00:15:34.810
The ability to have sort of
a door to shut four walls

00:15:34.810 --> 00:15:37.130
that surround you being in your own space

00:15:37.130 --> 00:15:39.710
I think that was important
to people because your mind

00:15:39.710 --> 00:15:41.260
can finally rest.

00:15:41.260 --> 00:15:44.540
Tyler Antonin is the program
manager with Citynet,

00:15:44.540 --> 00:15:47.170
the nonprofit, the city
contracted with to run the site,

00:15:47.170 --> 00:15:50.220
which sits adjacent to a
parking lot less than two miles

00:15:50.220 --> 00:15:51.720
from downtown Riverside.

00:15:51.720 --> 00:15:55.070
Since launching in March,
2020, the site with 30 units

00:15:55.070 --> 00:15:59.640
has served 160 people moving
43 into permanent housing.

00:15:59.640 --> 00:16:01.410
You don't know what's
gonna happen on the street.

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:04.153
Coming in here I had my own
place which was a lot bigger

00:16:04.153 --> 00:16:05.260
than my tent.

00:16:05.260 --> 00:16:07.730
Lisa Care was living on
the streets in a tent

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:10.740
for two years before Citynet
offered her a pallet shelter

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.740
and eventually found her an apartment.

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:15.640
She says the privacy and
security offered was enough

00:16:15.640 --> 00:16:18.360
to bring her off the street
when often a big group

00:16:18.360 --> 00:16:19.760
living shelter wouldn't.

00:16:19.760 --> 00:16:21.370
There is no comparison whatsoever.

00:16:21.370 --> 00:16:23.590
This place is like living
in one bedroom mansion

00:16:23.590 --> 00:16:25.306
or something, as opposed to the shelter.

00:16:25.306 --> 00:16:27.600
I'd take my chances on the
street before I go back

00:16:27.600 --> 00:16:28.530
to the shelter.

00:16:28.530 --> 00:16:31.530
We saw that over 80 percent
indicated that if they had

00:16:31.530 --> 00:16:34.270
an opportunity to have their own space,

00:16:34.270 --> 00:16:35.920
they would accept shelter.

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.790
Whether it might be for
mental health purposes

00:16:38.790 --> 00:16:41.910
or just really wanting
that own personal space.

00:16:41.910 --> 00:16:44.730
We found that our population was more open

00:16:44.730 --> 00:16:47.110
and we're really excited to report

00:16:47.110 --> 00:16:48.611
that it has been a success.

00:16:48.611 --> 00:16:52.360
Hafsa Kaka is Riverside's
Homeless Solutions officer

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.610
and says that while she
believes more permanent

00:16:54.610 --> 00:16:57.770
supportive housing is what will
ultimately end homelessness

00:16:57.770 --> 00:17:00.230
that transitional options
like pallet shelters

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:01.930
actually help achieve that goal.

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:03.850
It's not one size fits all.

00:17:03.850 --> 00:17:05.990
Some of the individuals
who maybe struggled

00:17:05.990 --> 00:17:08.420
with getting into permanent
housing were able to find

00:17:08.420 --> 00:17:11.180
this as a pathway, filling
out paperwork, document,

00:17:11.180 --> 00:17:14.590
ready ID, all of those same
some individuals struggle with.

00:17:14.590 --> 00:17:17.130
And so by having the shelter

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:19.960
and the shelter case management,
they're able to get ready

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:20.973
for that step.

00:17:21.835 --> 00:17:25.250
There are even lower cost
options used in other cities

00:17:25.250 --> 00:17:28.440
like 3D printed homes in
Austin and Mexico City

00:17:28.440 --> 00:17:32.400
produced by a startup
for 4,000 dollars in 24 hours.

00:17:32.400 --> 00:17:36.070
And in 2016 Reason profiled
a local artist constructing

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:39.850
tiny homes for the homeless
for only 1,200 dollars a piece.

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:42.060
Instead of partnering
with him and offering land

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:44.680
for the homes, the city
seized in a pound at them.

00:17:44.680 --> 00:17:47.180
The more than billion dollars
that the city has borrowed

00:17:47.180 --> 00:17:49.050
could be used to build shelters like this,

00:17:49.050 --> 00:17:51.170
but the city government
has failed to allocate

00:17:51.170 --> 00:17:54.160
much of the money to
fast temporary solutions.

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:56.680
But other homeless service
providers and activists

00:17:56.680 --> 00:17:58.670
and the editorial board of the LA times

00:17:58.670 --> 00:18:01.060
have expressed concern
that judge Carter's order

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:02.970
will derail the plan
to put homeless people

00:18:02.970 --> 00:18:05.850
into permanent housing by
prioritizing substandard,

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:07.210
temporary shelters.

00:18:07.210 --> 00:18:10.250
Amy Turk points out that while
temporary sites are cheap

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:12.350
to build, they can be expensive to run.

00:18:12.350 --> 00:18:15.030
With one parking lot
campground, the city administers

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:18.580
costing more than 2,600 dollars
a month per person.

00:18:18.580 --> 00:18:20.730
Can't just set up a
shelter without knowing

00:18:20.730 --> 00:18:23.310
how you're going to get
people into permanent housing,

00:18:23.310 --> 00:18:25.610
because then people will just
stay in your interim shelter

00:18:25.610 --> 00:18:26.910
for a very long time.

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:31.850
It is more costly to run a
shelter with 24 hour staffing

00:18:31.850 --> 00:18:35.560
and security, you cannot
look at a place like Skid Row

00:18:35.560 --> 00:18:38.490
and think like, "Oh, we don't
need an immediate response."

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:42.270
But we can't at the expense
of perhaps clawing back money

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.620
that we've designated
for permanent housing

00:18:44.620 --> 00:18:47.900
or not having enough for
the permanent housing side.

00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:50.340
Turk is CEO of the
Downtown Women's Center.

00:18:50.340 --> 00:18:52.600
Another Skid Row service
provider that helps

00:18:52.600 --> 00:18:55.600
homeless women find food
services and housing.

00:18:55.600 --> 00:18:58.090
Turk's group has backed
the housing first approach

00:18:58.090 --> 00:19:00.370
championed by the city
and owns two buildings

00:19:00.370 --> 00:19:03.010
offering permanent supportive
housing in Skid Row.

00:19:03.010 --> 00:19:07.550
They found housing for 79
women and 124 children in 2019

00:19:07.550 --> 00:19:09.610
according to their annual report.

00:19:09.610 --> 00:19:12.420
Turk says the goal should be
to move people experiencing

00:19:12.420 --> 00:19:15.040
homelessness into permanent
supportive housing

00:19:15.040 --> 00:19:18.230
as Downtown Women's Center
helped Suzette Shaw accomplish.

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:20.820
People living in Skid Row
are living in less than

00:19:20.820 --> 00:19:22.520
third world living conditions.

00:19:22.520 --> 00:19:25.830
You're always in a state of
survivor mode, whoever you are,

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:27.860
and wherever you came from.

00:19:27.860 --> 00:19:31.140
A homeless rights activist,
Shaw has lived on Skid Row

00:19:31.140 --> 00:19:33.530
for more than eight years
though she never slept

00:19:33.530 --> 00:19:36.390
on the streets instead
living in single occupancy,

00:19:36.390 --> 00:19:37.940
hotel rooms, and shelters.

00:19:37.940 --> 00:19:39.610
And then the Downtown Women's Center

00:19:39.610 --> 00:19:42.204
helped her find a permanent
place in this building.

00:19:42.204 --> 00:19:46.400
Having my own home now and
space that I can create

00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:49.400
and make that of my own
home and slowly working

00:19:49.400 --> 00:19:51.240
to a sense of normalcy.

00:19:51.240 --> 00:19:53.720
I closed that door and it's my sanctuary.

00:19:53.720 --> 00:19:54.730
You know what I'm saying?

00:19:54.730 --> 00:19:57.290
But while Shaw is a notable success story,

00:19:57.290 --> 00:19:59.810
LA's homeless population continues to grow

00:19:59.810 --> 00:20:02.440
year over year and three
quarters of the city's

00:20:02.440 --> 00:20:04.450
homeless remained without shelter.

00:20:04.450 --> 00:20:07.270
But we should not leave
this deadly status quo.

00:20:07.270 --> 00:20:10.580
All of the housing that
we could possibly do

00:20:10.580 --> 00:20:12.513
would be the correct response.

00:20:16.930 --> 00:20:19.020
High percentage of LA's
homeless population

00:20:19.020 --> 00:20:21.500
has mental illness and
substance abuse problems.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:24.290
And LA County has concluded
it has fewer than half

00:20:24.290 --> 00:20:26.600
the available mental health
beds necessary to serve

00:20:26.600 --> 00:20:27.530
as population.

00:20:27.530 --> 00:20:29.530
The judge has ordered
directs the city and county

00:20:29.530 --> 00:20:32.270
to spend more of the funds
to expand mental health

00:20:32.270 --> 00:20:35.280
and substance abuse
services within 90 days.

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:37.740
The substance abuse and
mental health services

00:20:37.740 --> 00:20:39.230
are atrocious.

00:20:39.230 --> 00:20:41.670
So by requiring the county
to actually treat people

00:20:41.670 --> 00:20:44.333
who need treatment, I
mean, it's life-changing.

00:20:45.260 --> 00:20:47.450
But there's also a significant
percentage of people

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:50.550
who aren't severely mentally
ill or addicted to drugs

00:20:50.550 --> 00:20:52.130
at the time they become homeless,

00:20:52.130 --> 00:20:53.730
they might be living at the margin,

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:56.160
pushed out of housing by a
combination of high rents

00:20:56.160 --> 00:20:57.880
and inability to navigate

00:20:57.880 --> 00:20:59.840
the housing assistance bureaucracy.

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:03.317
The city exacerbated this
problem on Skid Row in the 1950s

00:21:03.317 --> 00:21:07.040
and 1960s, by condemning and
demolishing over the course

00:21:07.040 --> 00:21:10.320
of a decade, half of the
existing single occupancy hotels

00:21:10.320 --> 00:21:11.770
that housed in many of the neighborhoods,

00:21:11.770 --> 00:21:13.883
extremely low income residents.

00:21:13.883 --> 00:21:17.120
UCLA economist William Yu
is the latest researcher

00:21:17.120 --> 00:21:20.350
to find a strong correlation
between high housing prices

00:21:20.350 --> 00:21:23.330
and homelessness in a
survey of American cities.

00:21:23.330 --> 00:21:25.960
California is home to some
of the least affordable

00:21:25.960 --> 00:21:27.850
urban housing markets in the country,

00:21:27.850 --> 00:21:30.910
including the LA Metro area,
according to the US census

00:21:30.910 --> 00:21:33.230
department's American community survey.

00:21:33.230 --> 00:21:36.420
Accounting survey found that
60% of newly homeless people

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:39.140
cite economic hardship
as the leading factor

00:21:39.140 --> 00:21:40.600
in their lack of housing.

00:21:40.600 --> 00:21:43.880
And that two thirds became homeless
while living in LA County.

00:21:43.880 --> 00:21:45.670
The approach of local and state officials

00:21:45.670 --> 00:21:48.820
has largely been to promote
measures like rent control

00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:51.550
and mandating low cost
housing and new construction.

00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:54.080
But market urbanists have
long said that housing

00:21:54.080 --> 00:21:56.830
is simply too expensive to
build because of zoning,

00:21:56.830 --> 00:21:59.120
permitting an onerous over-regulation.

00:21:59.120 --> 00:22:00.760
There's this whole line of thought.

00:22:00.760 --> 00:22:03.040
We have a simple supply
and demand problem,

00:22:03.040 --> 00:22:06.330
and we need to cut you out of the picture

00:22:06.330 --> 00:22:09.593
and just let people build housing.

00:22:09.593 --> 00:22:12.220
There's always truth, some
truth and there's always

00:22:12.220 --> 00:22:13.300
a little bit of both sides of this.

00:22:13.300 --> 00:22:15.230
You know, you just make it
really easy to create supply.

00:22:15.230 --> 00:22:17.060
You may end up with just a lot of housing

00:22:17.060 --> 00:22:17.893
that's hard to reach.

00:22:17.893 --> 00:22:21.610
If there's so much supply,
even the expensive housing

00:22:21.610 --> 00:22:23.160
would become cheaper, right?

00:22:23.160 --> 00:22:27.630
In theory, but that's often
not what we see in practice.

00:22:27.630 --> 00:22:29.930
We see wealthier people
moving to Los Angeles,

00:22:29.930 --> 00:22:32.550
and that's also a recipe
for gentrification.

00:22:32.550 --> 00:22:35.280
But several studies have
demonstrated that increasing

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:37.950
the housing supply would bring
down the price of housing

00:22:37.950 --> 00:22:38.783
for the poor.

00:22:38.783 --> 00:22:41.610
A journal of urban economic
study of the bay area

00:22:41.610 --> 00:22:44.780
found local land use
regulations are closely linked

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:46.720
to the value of houses sold.

00:22:46.720 --> 00:22:48.840
Brookings Institute study of California

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:51.040
found cities with less restrictive zoning

00:22:51.040 --> 00:22:54.470
and large populations issued
more multi-family permits.

00:22:54.470 --> 00:22:56.980
And a federal reserve
study found that in metros

00:22:56.980 --> 00:22:58.950
where demand for housing is high,

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:02.270
more regulations correlated
to almost double the increase

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:03.590
in housing prices.

00:23:03.590 --> 00:23:06.420
Even regulation meant to
directly address the problem

00:23:06.420 --> 00:23:09.250
by mandating affordable
housing caused prices

00:23:09.250 --> 00:23:13.830
to rise two to 3 percent faster
according to a 2009 HUD study.

00:23:13.830 --> 00:23:15.910
Since the housing market
structural problems

00:23:15.910 --> 00:23:18.830
won't be fixed anytime soon,
the homeless population

00:23:18.830 --> 00:23:20.410
is likely to keep growing.

00:23:20.410 --> 00:23:22.720
And mayor Garcetti may
be headed out the door

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:25.780
after reportedly being offered
an ambassador ship to India

00:23:25.780 --> 00:23:27.420
from the Biden administration.

00:23:27.420 --> 00:23:30.740
But his successor will have
to decide whether to continue

00:23:30.740 --> 00:23:33.570
on the course he set by
fighting a legal battle

00:23:33.570 --> 00:23:35.580
while spending taxpayer
money on six figure

00:23:35.580 --> 00:23:38.400
permanent housing units or to shift course

00:23:38.400 --> 00:23:40.840
in favor of cheaper,
faster emergency shelter

00:23:40.840 --> 00:23:43.800
to more immediately address
the deteriorating conditions

00:23:43.800 --> 00:23:45.880
in the city's public spaces.

00:23:45.880 --> 00:23:48.980
Judge Carter ordered the city
to offer some form of shelter

00:23:48.980 --> 00:23:51.360
for everyone on Skid Row by October.

00:23:51.360 --> 00:23:55.030
The ninth circuit court overrode
that order on June 10th,

00:23:55.030 --> 00:23:57.800
pending an appeal to be heard on July 7th.

00:23:57.800 --> 00:24:00.970
How could anybody be
blind to the suffering?

00:24:00.970 --> 00:24:03.620
Really it's all a matter of when people

00:24:03.620 --> 00:24:05.810
don't have a roof over their heads.

00:24:05.810 --> 00:24:09.000
It becomes very survival of the fittest

00:24:09.000 --> 00:24:12.390
it's beyond a disaster
and yet the officials

00:24:12.390 --> 00:24:14.780
seem to be allowing it to get worse

00:24:14.780 --> 00:24:16.770
rather than intervening.

00:24:16.770 --> 00:24:19.040
Judge Carter sees the current situation

00:24:19.040 --> 00:24:21.740
as not only a failure of
current city leadership,

00:24:21.740 --> 00:24:25.020
but a result of decades of
government neglect and abuse.

00:24:25.020 --> 00:24:27.360
Starting with the city
creating and sustaining

00:24:27.360 --> 00:24:30.180
the squalor of Skid Row by
making it a containment zone

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:34.420
in 1976, condemning and demolishing
thousands of inexpensive

00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:37.580
single room occupancy
hotels using eminent domain

00:24:37.580 --> 00:24:40.950
to seize homes in poor communities
for highway construction

00:24:40.950 --> 00:24:43.830
and driving up housing prices
through exclusionary zoning

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:45.410
and excessive permitting.

00:24:45.410 --> 00:24:48.360
All policies that he says
were designed to segregate

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:51.170
and disenfranchise, the
costs of which had fallen

00:24:51.170 --> 00:24:54.690
disproportionately on black
Americans who make up 42 percent

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.250
of the homeless population,

00:24:56.250 --> 00:24:59.980
despite being only 7 percent of
the city's population.

00:24:59.980 --> 00:25:03.500
But you're talking about
400 years of racism

00:25:03.500 --> 00:25:05.870
that has contributed to homelessness.

00:25:05.870 --> 00:25:08.430
So how do we undo that overnight?

00:25:08.430 --> 00:25:11.310
Is the big moral question of our time.

00:25:11.310 --> 00:25:15.170
At the heart of this is
really the systemic failures

00:25:15.170 --> 00:25:18.470
that are embedded within
our system that have allowed

00:25:18.470 --> 00:25:22.120
red lining and the
displacement for Suzette Shaw

00:25:22.120 --> 00:25:25.760
and others to no longer exist
in their former communities

00:25:25.760 --> 00:25:27.630
and have to come to a
place called Skid Row

00:25:27.630 --> 00:25:29.340
because they had no other place to go.

00:25:29.340 --> 00:25:31.980
I feel that it's wrong
to criminalize homeless.

00:25:31.980 --> 00:25:35.740
Yes, absolutely, but my
staff deserve to feel safe.

00:25:35.740 --> 00:25:38.030
My clients deserve to feel safe.

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:42.310
It's not about criminalizing
them, it's about helping them.

00:25:42.310 --> 00:25:45.100
The judge's initial order was very fair,

00:25:45.100 --> 00:25:48.030
especially towards the homeless population

00:25:48.030 --> 00:25:49.400
who need that funding.

00:25:49.400 --> 00:25:52.280
It's just not fair for the
city to take their time

00:25:52.280 --> 00:25:54.980
and do their process
where it hasn't worked

00:25:54.980 --> 00:25:55.990
in the last 10 years.

00:25:55.990 --> 00:25:57.670
The mayor has been in
office for eight years

00:25:57.670 --> 00:26:00.830
and there's never been a decline
in the homeless population.

00:26:00.830 --> 00:26:04.690
How can you say a judge is slowing me down

00:26:04.690 --> 00:26:09.690
when we've developed 641 very expensive,

00:26:09.810 --> 00:26:14.810
slow to develop units while
5,700 Angelenos have died

00:26:15.840 --> 00:26:16.673
on our streets.

00:26:16.673 --> 00:26:19.730
The county and the city
appealing the judges ordered

00:26:19.730 --> 00:26:23.150
to immediately put a roof
over everybody on Skid Row.

00:26:23.150 --> 00:26:26.020
Add that to the record of
corralling and containment

00:26:26.020 --> 00:26:28.580
because all that the
city and county are doing

00:26:28.580 --> 00:26:31.630
is building the case against themselves

00:26:31.630 --> 00:26:35.963
for not wanting to address
homelessness on Skid Row.

